# Overview

[ops safety protocol — production operation guard]

This rule applies when:
- operating live SaaS systems
- handling customer data
- monitoring payments and billing
- handling incidents, failures, or anomalies
- optimizing infrastructure cost

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1. incident-first mindset (critical)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
When an issue is detected:
- prioritize system stability over feature delivery
- stop feature rollout immediately
- identify blast radius first
- isolate the fault domain before fixing

Never:
- patch blindly
- deploy speculative fixes
- stack multiple fixes in one release

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2. failure containment strategy
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
All failures must:
- degrade gracefully
- never cascade across:
  - billing
  - auth
  - workspace resolution
  - webhook processing

If a subsystem fails:
- fail closed (deny sensitive operations)
- provide safe UI fallback
- log structured error

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
3. billing & revenue anomaly detection (critical)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Always monitor:
- payment success vs failure rate
- webhook delivery failure
- duplicated events
- entitlement mismatches
- sudden drops in conversion

When anomaly detected:
- freeze billing rollout
- confirm webhook + DB state
- verify reconciliation logic

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
4. data integrity & auditability
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
All critical operations must:
- write audit logs:
  - billing
  - credit usage
  - entitlement change
  - workspace role change

Never:
- silently mutate billing or entitlement state
- modify credits without traceable logs

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
5. rollback-first deployment strategy
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Every operational change must:
- be rollbackable
- avoid irreversible DB mutations
- preserve data consistency during rollback

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
6. hotfix discipline
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Hotfix rules:
- minimal diff only
- no refactoring
- no style changes
- no feature additions
- only bug isolation + fix

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
7. monitoring & alert signals
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Critical alert signals:
- webhook failure rate > 1%
- payment success drop > 5%
- build error spike
- workspace resolution failure
- auth callback failure

All alerts must:
- trigger investigation
- block new deployments

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
8. cost control & burn protection
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Continuously monitor:
- OpenAI API token usage
- DB row growth rate
- event log accumulation
- webhook retry loops

If cost anomaly detected:
- throttle
- cap
- degrade non-essential features

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
9. customer support workflow guard
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
When user reports an issue:
- never mutate production data blindly
- reproduce in staging or via logs
- confirm before correction
- log intervention

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
10. production debugging discipline
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Never:
- enable verbose logging in prod
- expose raw errors to users
- print stack traces publicly

Use:
- controlled error reporting
- masked logs
- trace IDs

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
11. security escalation protocol
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
If security anomaly suspected:
- freeze billing
- revoke suspicious tokens
- rotate secrets
- isolate affected subsystems

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
12. operational success criteria
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Operations are healthy only if:
- payment flow stable
- webhook delivery reliable
- entitlement gating consistent
- cost within thresholds
- no silent failures

